---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Scientific Rationale">
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Overview"
    title="Scientific Rationale"
    intro="The scientific motivation for this international workshop."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <article class="rationale-card">
        <div class="rationale-content prose">
          <p class="lead-text">
            Understanding how stars form, evolve and die is one of the main goals of modern astrophysics. Asteroseismology, the study of stellar internal oscillations, offers a unique window into the deep interiors of stars. Space missions such as Kepler, GAIA, TESS and high-resolution spectroscopic observations have revolutionized our understanding of stellar physics by detecting pressure and gravity modes. Interpreting these observations using modelling allows us to probe internal stellar processes from the core to the surface and infer the basic parameters such as effective temperature, surface gravity, differential rotation, chemical composition, and mixing. The chemically peculiar (CP) stars are a group of main-sequence B-, A-, and F-type stars with unusual surface elemental abundances and abnormal spectral line strengths. The CP stars are important testbeds for understanding the interactions among pulsation, rotation, binarity, and chemical inhomogeneities in intermediate-mass stars. Star clusters provide ideal laboratories for testing the stellar evolution theory. Because the members of the star clusters share a common age, distance and chemical composition, their position in the H-R diagram offers unique constraints for the stellar modeling. The star cluster provides masses, luminosities and ages that can reduce the degeneracies in stellar modelling; however, asteroseismology reveals the internal physics that governs the evolutionary path of the cluster members. Despite the transformative successes of both the cluster and asteroseismology, particularly of the CP stars, the two fields have largely evolved in parallel and their synergy represents a natural step in astrophysics.
          </p>

          <div class="aim-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

          <p class="aim-text">
            The main aim of organizing this workshop is to foster synergistic research between asteroseismology and stellar cluster studies, making full use of existing ground- and space-based observational facilities to develop a strategy for future missions and datasets. The objective of this effort is to bridge the gap between the two communities and identify the most promising directions for joint research in the coming decades by bringing together experts from around the world to discuss and explore future collaborative opportunities.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .rationale-card {
    max-width: 880px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    background: #ffffff;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    box-shadow: 0 4px 20px -2px rgba(16, 29, 71, 0.05);
    padding: clamp(1.8rem, 3.5vw, 2.75rem);
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  .rationale-content {
    color: var(--ink-700);
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.18vw, 1.15rem);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .lead-text {
    margin: 0;
    text-align: justify;
    text-justify: inter-word;
  }

  .aim-divider {
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--line);
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
  }

  .aim-text {
    margin: 0;
    text-align: justify;
    text-justify: inter-word;
    color: var(--navy-950);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .rationale-card {
      padding: 1.4rem 1.1rem;
    }
    .lead-text,
    .aim-text {
      text-align: left;
    }
  }
</style>
